00:01:03.128 --> 00:01:04.564
Hi, Greta.
-Hi.

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:05.664
-I am so happy
you're here.

00:01:05.731 --> 00:01:06.899
I love this movie so much.

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:08.600
-Oh. Thank you. You saw it.

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:10.836
-I did see it.
Everybody has to see it.

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:11.837
-Yes.
-There's a moment --

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:13.073
Have you seen it --

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:15.475
much with, uh,
audiences full of people?

00:01:15.541 --> 00:01:16.943
-No, I haven't.
-You haven't.

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:19.011
-I really want
to watch it on the plane,

00:01:19.078 --> 00:01:20.313
but I think that would be weird.

00:01:20.379 --> 00:01:21.548
-Oh, yeah?

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:22.614
-Like if I did that.
-That's true. Right.

00:01:22.681 --> 00:01:23.850
-If you were watching it.

00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:25.684
-Yeah, if I was.
And crying at my own movie.

00:01:25.751 --> 00:01:27.053
-Yeah.
-I don't know, I feel like that

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.788
would be a little weird.
-It is a really good crier.

00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:30.022
And I should note,
this is not a movie

00:01:30.089 --> 00:01:33.226
that is tragic in any way,
but it is --

00:01:33.292 --> 00:01:34.527
-I don't know.
[ Sighs ]

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:35.828
-it's optimistically tragic.
-Soulful.

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:38.097
Optimistically, yeah. Tragic.
-Yeah.

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:39.165
-That's a good way
of putting it.

00:01:39.232 --> 00:01:40.633
-Yeah.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:41.834
-There's a moment in the film,
I think, you know,

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:43.235
and I don't want
to give anything away.

00:01:43.302 --> 00:01:44.637
I would want to see it
in a theater if I were you,

00:01:44.704 --> 00:01:46.339
because I feel like
half the audience

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:47.773
would just go like,
[Shakily] "huh?"

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:49.241
You know what I mean?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.308 --> 00:01:51.077
Like, that's what I did.
-I think I know what moment

00:01:51.144 --> 00:01:52.411
you mean towards the end,
maybe?

00:01:52.478 --> 00:01:53.847
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-Or just at the beginning.

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:55.314
Right?
Right off the bat.

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:57.416
-No, no, I had one at the end
that really got me.

00:01:57.483 --> 00:01:58.884
-Yeah.
And one of our producers,

00:01:58.951 --> 00:02:00.586
uh, who said, you know,

00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:02.321
Sarah said, "When I watched it,
just FYI,

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:05.458
I watched it in my office
and I fully cried at my desk,"

00:02:05.524 --> 00:02:06.826
and I was like,
"Hahaha, Sarah."

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:07.660
And I gave her a real hard time.

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:09.128
And then...

00:02:09.194 --> 00:02:11.330
And then I watched it,
and I fully cried at my desk.

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:12.464
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:12.531 --> 00:02:13.566
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:15.201
It can be a dehydrating
experience.

00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:16.735
-It's very dehydrating.

00:02:16.802 --> 00:02:18.071
Now, this, uh, this movie,

00:02:18.137 --> 00:02:21.974
uh, well, again, it's, uh --
-Made you cry.

00:02:22.041 --> 00:02:23.409
-Made me cry.
-Yeah.

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:25.911
-But you, uh, you play someone
who leaves a career

00:02:25.978 --> 00:02:27.947
when you're 12 years old
and moves to New York City,

00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:29.482
and it's about, uh, you know,

00:02:29.548 --> 00:02:30.916
someone you knew
when you were 12

00:02:30.983 --> 00:02:32.852
and sort of that reconnection
with somebody like this.

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:34.753
This is a wonderful film
that was written

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:36.822
and directed by Celine Song.
-Yep.

00:02:36.889 --> 00:02:38.490
-And how much of it
did you know?

00:02:38.557 --> 00:02:39.592
Did she fill you in?

00:02:39.658 --> 00:02:41.560
Was this autobiographical
for her?

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:43.830
-Yeah,
it actually happened to her.

00:02:43.897 --> 00:02:45.698
She -- The seed of the movie

00:02:45.764 --> 00:02:49.368
was when she was actually
sitting between her,

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:53.305
her husband, her Jewish husband,
who lives in New York with her,

00:02:53.372 --> 00:02:55.307
and her actual
childhood sweetheart

00:02:55.374 --> 00:02:57.643
who had come to visit her
from Korea.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.346
Um, and she said
that that moment felt

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:04.417
so, uh, hard to explain,
that it would take a whole movie

00:03:04.484 --> 00:03:05.918
just to explain
what that felt like.

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:07.686
-That's fantastic.
-Mm-hmm. Yeah.

00:03:07.753 --> 00:03:12.891
-Now, uh, your parents, uh,
they immigrated from Korea.

00:03:12.958 --> 00:03:14.994
-Yeah.
-And, uh, were they excited

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:16.495
that you were doing
a film like this?

00:03:16.562 --> 00:03:17.663
-No.
-Okay.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.498
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.565 --> 00:03:20.799
-They were sort of like. Why?

00:03:20.866 --> 00:03:22.367
-Yeah.
-But you --

00:03:22.434 --> 00:03:23.670
And one of the things
you had to take,

00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:26.405
you, uh, speak Korean,
but you had to --

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:27.873
You took it very seriously,
and you wanted to speak

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.575
Korean like somebody who left
when they were 12.

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:30.643
-Right. And that
was the whole thing.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:32.344
And that was part
of their doubt.

00:03:32.411 --> 00:03:33.746
They were like, you, you're --

00:03:33.813 --> 00:03:35.214
you're going to do
a full movie in Korean.

00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:36.916
-They thought you weren't
Korean enough to do it.

00:03:36.982 --> 00:03:38.617
-Yeah, they were
deeply concerned.

00:03:38.684 --> 00:03:40.085
They were like,
that's our daughter.

00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:41.587
You know, we can't let her
humiliate herself.

00:03:41.653 --> 00:03:43.155
-Did they, like,
call the director.

00:03:43.222 --> 00:03:44.223
And they're like, you've made
a terrible mistake.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:45.958
-Almost. Almost.
-Yeah.

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:48.294
-No, I mean that, the language
part of it, it is --

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:51.397
Okay. Anyone who speaks
two languages or is bicultural,

00:03:51.463 --> 00:03:54.333
that is such
a vulnerable thing to show.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.102
And language is so fluid.

00:03:56.169 --> 00:03:59.172
And, you know,
as you become Americanized,

00:03:59.238 --> 00:04:02.909
you -- you lose some
of your native language.

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:05.878
And what we did was we worked
with this incredible woman,

00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:07.346
Sharon Choi,

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:09.749
who a lot of people know
as Bong Joon ho's translator.

00:04:09.816 --> 00:04:11.751
-Oh, wow.
-She's amazing.

00:04:11.818 --> 00:04:14.487
And because she can
translate jokes on the spot

00:04:14.553 --> 00:04:16.855
and that, to me,
is a true master.

00:04:16.922 --> 00:04:18.024
-Yes. Of course.
-Yeah.

00:04:18.091 --> 00:04:19.791
So we worked together
to figure out,

00:04:19.858 --> 00:04:22.494
like, if what a New Yorker

00:04:22.561 --> 00:04:26.031
who's turned into sort of
like an annoying theater girl,

00:04:26.098 --> 00:04:29.168
like what her Korean
would sound like.

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:30.402
So maybe --
-That's really great.

00:04:30.469 --> 00:04:32.271
-Yes. It's very specific.

00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:35.040
You know, only the most annoying
Korean theater girls

00:04:35.107 --> 00:04:37.844
know what that is,
and we need to honor that.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:39.144
-Yeah.
-Representation --

00:04:39.211 --> 00:04:41.013
It's a very serious thing.
-Absolutely.

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:42.848
And I think they would let you
know if you came up short.

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:44.150
-They would definitely. Yes.

00:04:44.216 --> 00:04:45.585
-That's where the annoying
comes in.

00:04:45.651 --> 00:04:47.419
-Yeah. That's right.
-So when your parents,

00:04:47.486 --> 00:04:48.854
for example,
first saw the trailer,

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:51.857
did they think,
"Oh, my God, she did it."

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:54.494
-They texted me
when they saw the trailer.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:55.828
They said,

00:04:55.895 --> 00:04:57.296
"Why is this in Korean?"

00:04:57.363 --> 00:04:58.831
[ Laughter ]

00:04:58.898 --> 00:05:00.433
-So they just hadn't listened.
-Parents don't listen.

00:05:00.499 --> 00:05:02.134
-No they don't.
-They don't listen.

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:05.070
And now that I'm a parent,
I agree we don't listen.

00:05:05.137 --> 00:05:09.275
-Uh, so your boys are almost
the same age as my boys.

00:05:09.342 --> 00:05:10.843
Are they --
Do they know what you do?

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:13.412
Like mine are slowly coming to.
It's dawning on them.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:15.114
-Yeah, they don't
give a [bleep].

00:05:15.181 --> 00:05:16.516
-Okay.

00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:17.783
-Do yours?
-But I would imagine --

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.184
-They don't give a sh--

00:05:19.251 --> 00:05:21.620
No, they like that
people say hi to me.

00:05:21.687 --> 00:05:23.322
-[ Laughing ] Oh, okay.
-I think they like the idea.

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:24.723
-The bar is really low
for them.

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:25.991
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:27.526
Well I think they like,
live in New York

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:29.227
and they're like,
nobody says hi to anybody.

00:05:29.294 --> 00:05:30.529
-Yeah.
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:32.999
-Um, but you're I would
imagine more now, right?

00:05:33.065 --> 00:05:34.733
Because of this film.
Like, people must approach you

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.702
and I'm assuming
you're very nice.

00:05:36.769 --> 00:05:39.104
-Yes.
There is some crying, though,

00:05:39.171 --> 00:05:43.208
which might add to the confusion
and sort of like why my children

00:05:43.275 --> 00:05:44.943
would be even
more dismissive of everything.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:47.012
-People come up to you and are
so affected by the film.

00:05:47.079 --> 00:05:48.714
When they say hello,
they start to cry.

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:51.016
-Yeah, like imagine you're at --
you're at Trader Joe's

00:05:51.083 --> 00:05:52.685
and you're trying to buy cereal.
-Yeah.

00:05:52.752 --> 00:05:54.286
-And someone comes up
to you and they say,

00:05:54.353 --> 00:05:56.288
"Oh my God, like,
I just saw your movie."

00:05:56.355 --> 00:05:58.257
And you're thinking, great,
that's so wonderful.

00:05:58.323 --> 00:05:59.926
And then they immediately
start to bawl.

00:05:59.992 --> 00:06:01.728
-Yeah, I mean, I imagine
that's hard for you.

00:06:01.794 --> 00:06:04.497
I imagine that's so traumatic
for a seven and a four year old.

00:06:04.563 --> 00:06:06.065
-It is, it is, it is.

00:06:06.132 --> 00:06:08.467
And I hate to say that they've
sort of gotten used to it.

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:09.635
-Yeah.
-So.

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:11.070
-Oh that's --
But do they --

00:06:11.136 --> 00:06:12.638
I mean, obviously
you haven't made a lot of work

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:14.940
that is like very friendly
to seven and four year olds.

00:06:15.007 --> 00:06:17.042
Have they like --
Did they watch

00:06:17.109 --> 00:06:18.977
you even show them the trailer
for something like they.

00:06:19.044 --> 00:06:21.513
-Well, they saw --
My oldest, Apollo, is --

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:23.248
is a very serious dude.

00:06:23.315 --> 00:06:26.618
And he, uh, saw the trailer
and he was completely silent

00:06:26.685 --> 00:06:28.788
the whole time
and expressionless.

00:06:28.855 --> 00:06:31.256
And when it finished,
he just said,

00:06:31.323 --> 00:06:32.958
"Looks like a good movie,"
and walked away.

00:06:33.025 --> 00:06:34.259
[ Laughter ]

00:06:34.326 --> 00:06:35.628
That's --
That's like, pretty good.

00:06:35.695 --> 00:06:36.963
In our household.
-That's pretty good. Yeah.

00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:38.597
-If one of my kids
said that to me, I would --

00:06:38.664 --> 00:06:40.532
I would start crying like I'd
just seen "Past Lives" again.

00:06:40.599 --> 00:06:41.700
-Yeah, basically.

00:06:41.767 --> 00:06:43.001
That would be
my "Past Lives" moment.

00:06:43.068 --> 00:06:44.570
If they were like,
"That's pretty good."

00:06:44.637 --> 00:06:46.505
-They also do this thing.
I don't know if you have this.

00:06:46.572 --> 00:06:48.674
I mean it --
Winning awards

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:52.745
is a very new kind of thing
for me as a concept.

00:06:52.812 --> 00:06:55.848
But how are your children
with the actual physical award?

00:06:55.915 --> 00:06:58.584
Because my kids are so critical,

00:06:58.651 --> 00:07:00.018
they want to know
they're like, what is that?

00:07:00.085 --> 00:07:02.421
They're like, is that Sapphire?
-Yeah.

00:07:02.488 --> 00:07:06.458
-Like, no, it's plastic.
They're like, "Oh, okay."

00:07:06.525 --> 00:07:09.528
-Not to not to name drop.
We've got one Emmy in the house.

00:07:09.595 --> 00:07:10.662
-Yes.
-Yeah.

00:07:10.729 --> 00:07:12.598
-Um, I won it.
Not my wife and, uh.

00:07:12.664 --> 00:07:13.933
-Important.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:13.999 --> 00:07:15.034
Important to get out there.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:15.101 --> 00:07:16.402
-But, uh,
they did have a moment

00:07:16.469 --> 00:07:17.603
where they were like,
they've just noticed it,

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:19.104
and they're like "Real gold"?
-Right.

00:07:19.171 --> 00:07:20.239
And I'm like, "No."
And they're like, [Groans].

00:07:20.305 --> 00:07:21.373
Yeah, like, [ Sighs ].

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:22.875
-They're like,
"Maybe one day, Daddy."

00:07:22.941 --> 00:07:24.076
-Yeah. I'm like,
what are you --

00:07:24.143 --> 00:07:26.078
And if it was real gold,
what would you do?

00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:27.546
-Melt it down.
-Yeah.

00:07:27.613 --> 00:07:30.349
They're immediately like,
"Your awards are short term.

00:07:30.416 --> 00:07:31.484
This is money, Dad."

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:32.952
-Exactly.
So practical.

00:07:33.018 --> 00:07:34.753
-It's so practical.
-Yeah.

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:36.121
-It's just such a beautiful
film.

00:07:36.188 --> 00:07:37.490
And congratulations...
Thank you.

00:07:37.556 --> 00:07:40.192
-...on, uh, your Critics Choice
nomination, and...

00:07:40.259 --> 00:07:42.127
-Yeah.
-Well deserved.

00:07:42.194 --> 00:07:43.429
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:43.496 --> 00:07:47.099
-You know, it is.
But can I just say

00:07:47.166 --> 00:07:49.602
that I right now,
in this moment, I'm --

00:07:49.668 --> 00:07:51.937
I'm just thinking about
something totally different.

00:07:52.004 --> 00:07:53.773
And I'm carrying around
a lot of shame.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:56.041
I just learned
that I am responsible

00:07:56.108 --> 00:07:58.110
for committing a heinous crime

00:07:58.177 --> 00:08:00.679
because there's something
called a Kimjang,

00:08:00.746 --> 00:08:04.016
which is this traditional event
where back thousands,

00:08:04.083 --> 00:08:05.918
maybe centuries
of years ago,

00:08:05.985 --> 00:08:08.420
Koreans would gather together
as a community

00:08:08.487 --> 00:08:12.157
with all of their hard-earned
resources and make kimchi.

00:08:12.224 --> 00:08:14.526
Right. So we do this event.
And this year,

00:08:14.593 --> 00:08:18.030
it happened to fall
right after the Academy gala.

00:08:18.097 --> 00:08:21.400
And Charles Melton came
with his mother, Sandra Oh.

00:08:21.467 --> 00:08:23.435
-Very good.
-All the fancy Koreans.

00:08:23.502 --> 00:08:25.137
And I just learned

00:08:25.203 --> 00:08:28.040
that I single-handedly
ruined the kimchi.

00:08:28.106 --> 00:08:29.708
-No, how?

00:08:29.775 --> 00:08:31.743
-I didn't wash it enough.

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:33.412
-Oh my God.
-I know. But it's like --

00:08:33.479 --> 00:08:35.314
-I'm gonna act like I know
that's bad.

00:08:35.381 --> 00:08:37.283
-Yes, should be like,
"Oh, my God."

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:38.750
-You've gotta wash it enough.
-Yes.

00:08:38.817 --> 00:08:40.452
No, but, Seth,
this is really bad.

00:08:40.519 --> 00:08:42.888
It's basically like I've killed
the whole village.

00:08:42.955 --> 00:08:44.590
-Oh, my God.
-Yes.

00:08:44.657 --> 00:08:47.426
And it's like the village has
decided, like, send her away.

00:08:47.492 --> 00:08:49.428
Let her be an actor.
It's safer for everyone.

00:08:49.495 --> 00:08:50.829
-You've been cast out?

00:08:50.896 --> 00:08:51.963
-Yes.
-Oh, my God.

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.398
-I've been fired.
-Oh, my God.

00:08:53.465 --> 00:08:55.368
Well, I hope
they let you back in.

00:08:55.434 --> 00:08:57.035
Maybe if they -- if they haven't
seen this movie and they see it,

00:08:57.102 --> 00:08:58.137
I think they're going to
let you back in.

00:08:58.204 --> 00:08:59.438
-You think so?
-I do.

00:08:59.505 --> 00:09:00.606
-Okay.
-Thank you for being here.

00:09:00.672 --> 00:09:01.841
Will you, please,
come back soon?

00:09:01.907 --> 00:09:03.308
-I would love to come back.
-Oh, my God.

00:09:03.375 --> 00:09:04.877
Greta Lee, everyone.
"Past Lives"

00:09:04.944 --> 00:09:06.645
is available everywhere now.
Go and see it.

00:09:06.712 --> 00:09:09.015
We'll be right back
with chef Rick Martinez.

